# SEO Plan for New Pages

Every new page or route added to this site **must** follow the SEO plan documented in `docs/seo-implementation.md`. The checklist below covers the minimum requirements.

## 1. Metadata (required on every page)

Export a `metadata` object (or `generateMetadata` for dynamic routes) with:

- **title**: Keyword-rich, targeting a specific search query. Use the layout template (`%s | Hackathon Starter Kit`) by keeping the title short.
- **description**: 150-160 characters. Include the primary keyword naturally within the first sentence.
- **alternates.canonical**: Full canonical URL for the page (`${BASE_URL}/your-route`).
- **openGraph**: At minimum `title`, `description`, and `url`.
- **twitter**: At minimum `title` and `description`.

Example pattern (see any existing `app/playbook/*/page.tsx` for reference):

```ts
export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: "Your Keyword-Rich Title Here",
  description: "150-char description with primary keyword early in the sentence.",
  alternates: {
    canonical: `${process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL ?? "https://thehackathonplaybook.dev"}/your-route`,
  },
  openGraph: {
    title: "Your Title",
    description: "Your description.",
    url: `${process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL ?? "https://thehackathonplaybook.dev"}/your-route`,
  },
  twitter: {
    title: "Your Title",
    description: "Your description.",
  },
};
```

## 2. Sitemap (auto-handled for data-driven routes)

The sitemap at `app/sitemap.ts` dynamically imports from:
- `lib/playbook.ts` (playbook phases)
- `lib/non-coder-sections.ts` (non-coder sub-pages)
- `lib/non-coder-skills.ts` (skill detail pages)
- `lib/blog.ts` (blog posts)

**If your new page is data-driven** (i.e., its slug comes from one of these files), adding an entry to the data file is sufficient; the sitemap picks it up automatically.

**If your new page is a standalone route** (e.g., `app/about/page.tsx`), you must add it manually to `app/sitemap.ts`.

## 3. JSON-LD Structured Data (required for content-heavy pages)

Use the `<JsonLd>` component from `components/json-ld.tsx` to add structured data:

- **Blog posts**: Use `Article` + `BreadcrumbList` schemas (see `app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx`).
- **How-to or guide pages**: Use `HowTo` schema if the content describes a step-by-step process.
- **FAQ-style content**: Use `FAQPage` schema for pages with question/answer pairs.
- **List pages**: Use `CollectionPage` + `ItemList` (see `app/blog/page.tsx`).

Not every page needs JSON-LD, but any page with educational, guide, or FAQ content should have it.

## 4. Blog Posts

When adding a new blog post:
1. Add the post data to `lib/blog.ts` (the `BLOG_POSTS` array).
2. Include `slug`, `title`, `description`, `date`, `readingTime`, `keywords`, and `content`.
3. The `app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx` template handles rendering, metadata, and JSON-LD automatically.
4. The sitemap picks up new posts automatically.
5. Choose a slug that matches a target search query (e.g., `how-to-win-hackathons` targets "how to win hackathons").

## 5. Semantic HTML

- Use a single `<h1>` per page.
- Use `<h2>` for major sections, `<h3>` for subsections.
- Use `<article>` for blog posts and self-contained content.
- Use `<nav>` for navigation elements.
- Use `<section>` for thematic groupings.

## 6. Internal Linking

New pages should link to related existing pages (playbook phases, blog posts, non-coder sections) where relevant. Internal links help search engines discover content and pass authority between pages.

## 7. Blog Content Blocks

When adding a new blog post, use the `blocks` array (instead of plain `paragraphs`) to create visually rich content. Available block types are defined as the `ContentBlock` union type in `lib/blog.ts`:

- **`paragraph`**: Standard text paragraph.
- **`callout`**: Highlighted box with icon. Variants: `tip`, `warning`, `info`, `success`.
- **`stat-row`**: Grid of key metrics (value + label pairs).
- **`step-list`**: Numbered vertical timeline of steps (title + description).
- **`quote`**: Styled blockquote with optional attribution.
- **`pro-con`**: Two-column do/don't comparison.
- **`code-snippet`**: Code block with syntax label, filename, and copy button.
- **`checklist`**: Interactive checklist with progress bar.
- **`link-card`**: Internal navigation card with title, description, href, and optional tag.

Use a mix of 3-5 different block types per article to break up walls of text and keep readers engaged.

## Quick Reference

| What you're adding | Sitemap | Metadata | JSON-LD |
|---|---|---|---|
| New playbook phase | Add to `lib/playbook.ts` | Required | Optional (HowTo if step-by-step) |
| New non-coder section | Add to `lib/non-coder-sections.ts` | Required | Optional |
| New non-coder skill | Add to `lib/non-coder-skills.ts` | Required | Optional |
| New blog post | Add to `lib/blog.ts` | Auto-generated | Auto-generated |
| New standalone route | Add to `app/sitemap.ts` manually | Required | Recommended for content pages |
